  "progress_percent": 11,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "798b33a4-24fd-4a8f-b1b9-9a747594a8d6",
      "number": 1,
      "title": "Complexity & Big-O",
      "summary": "How to measure algorithm cost independently of hardware.",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "c5ab29b5-1583-46ad-aab4-a76019c1b2be",
          "number": 1,
          "title": "What Is an Algorithm?",
          "estimated_minutes": 8,
          "kind": "reading",
          "concept_tags": [
            "algorithm-definition",
            "why-asymptotic-analysis"
          ],
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "An algorithm is a finite sequence of well-defined steps that transforms an input into an output. The emphasis on *finite* matters: a procedure that never terminates is not an algorithm, however useful its partial results might be.",
              "id": "7590f69f-335f-4dc5-8a12-b1e75e68f619",
              "order": 0
            },
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "Every algorithm must also be unambiguous. Each step has to be precise enough that it could be carried out mechanically, without judgement calls from whoever is following it.",
              "id": "8786c7cf-36b6-4452-8032-f7a655a3067b",
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "61425239-2bbc-44bf-9b9d-adf9282ff405",
                "type": "mcq",
                "prompt": "Which of the following disqualifies a procedure from being an algorithm?",
                "options": [
                  "It uses randomness",
                  "It never terminates on some inputs",
                  "It is slow",
                  "It requires extra memory"
                ],
                "correct_index": 1,
                "explanation": "Finiteness is part of the definition. Randomized and slow procedures are still algorithms; a non-terminating one is not.",
                "concept_tag": "algorithm-definition",
                "difficulty": "easy",
                "source": "book",
                "hint": null,
                "model_answer": null
              },
              "id": "230f2ea3-56c5-466d-a824-69024e9db527",
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "We care about algorithms in the abstract, independent of the machine running them. This is why analysis focuses on how work grows with input size rather than on seconds elapsed on a particular laptop.",
              "id": "5c028644-38ae-4900-8536-4d66a5ee33df",
              "order": 3
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "04e6b9f0-5b86-49a0-b88e-688a36ce4bbf",
                "type": "open",
                "prompt": "In your own words, why is measuring an algorithm in seconds a poor way to compare two algorithms?",
                "options": null,
                "correct_index": null,
                "explanation": "Timing measures the environment as much as the algorithm; growth rate isolates the algorithm.",
                "concept_tag": "why-asymptotic-analysis",
                "difficulty": "medium",
                "source": "ai",
                "hint": "Think about what changes when you run the same code on a faster machine.",
                "model_answer": "Wall-clock time depends on hardware, language, compiler and load. Growth rate is a property of the algorithm itself and predicts behaviour as input scales."
              },
              "id": "c9e5394a-00ee-449c-98fb-79be1b0a824c",
              "order": 4
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "bfe71dd4-b220-4026-b3ee-c45b69110c8a",
          "number": 2,
          "title": "Growth of Functions",
          "estimated_minutes": 14,
          "kind": "reading",
          "concept_tags": [
            "growth-rates",
            "growth-hierarchy"
          ],
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "The running time of an algorithm is usually expressed as a function of input size n. Rather than count exact operations, we ask how that count grows as n increases.",
              "id": "2ddc158f-447c-4953-bd39-b6377246c260",
              "order": 0
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "b5a9edc6-65f5-4524-a92d-c0c07a986e5a",
                "type": "mcq",
                "prompt": "An algorithm performs 3n + 17 operations. What dominates as n grows large?",
                "options": [
                  "The constant 17",
                  "The coefficient 3",
                  "The term n",
                  "Nothing dominates"
                ],
                "correct_index": 2,
                "explanation": "As n grows, the linear term overwhelms the constant, and constant factors do not change the growth class.",
                "concept_tag": "growth-rates",
                "difficulty": "easy",
                "source": "ai",
                "hint": null,
                "model_answer": null
              },
              "id": "26ccd81d-a5ea-44d3-a4e4-fba784f89662",
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "Growth rates form a hierarchy. Constant time is followed by logarithmic, then linear, then linearithmic, then polynomial, then exponential and factorial time.",
              "id": "14347360-96a1-445b-9011-ad7c2f61a53c",
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "The gaps between these classes are enormous at scale. An O(n²) algorithm on a million elements does roughly a trillion operations; an O(n log n) algorithm does about twenty million.",
              "id": "ae2d28db-47b4-4707-97c7-0be9ea9e9389",
              "order": 3
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "afc711d7-00f7-454f-a091-72629af84e3d",
                "type": "mcq",
                "prompt": "Which ordering is correct from slowest-growing to fastest-growing?",
                "options": [
                  "O(log n), O(1), O(n), O(n²)",
                  "O(1), O(log n), O(n log n), O(n²)",
                  "O(1), O(n), O(log n), O(n²)",
                  "O(n), O(1), O(log n), O(2ⁿ)"
                ],
                "correct_index": 1,
                "explanation": "Constant, then logarithmic, then linear, then linearithmic, then polynomial.",
                "concept_tag": "growth-hierarchy",
                "difficulty": "medium",
                "source": "book",
                "hint": null,
                "model_answer": null
              },
              "id": "71738501-a633-49c1-9c9f-8ce6213937bf",
              "order": 4
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "de70c363-9316-4bcb-9a61-aed08ffc5be1",
          "number": 3,
          "title": "Big-O, Θ, and Ω Notation",
          "estimated_minutes": 18,
          "kind": "reading",
          "concept_tags": [
            "nested-loop-complexity",
            "logarithmic-scaling",
            "simplifying-big-o",
            "amortized-analysis"
          ],
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "An algorithm's time complexity describes how its runtime grows as input size n increases. Big-O notation captures the worst-case upper bound, discarding constants and lower-order terms — O(3n² + 2n + 7) simplifies to O(n²).",
              "id": "27b3b5ba-1d7a-4302-9bd4-158c8d83e477",
              "order": 0
            },
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "The hierarchy from fastest to slowest growth: O(1) → O(log n) → O(n) → O(n log n) → O(n²) → O(2ⁿ) → O(n!). Binary search achieves O(log n) because each step halves the search space — one million elements need at most ~20 comparisons.",
              "id": "aec287ea-ddcb-48c3-afae-e4fdc0c1ddf2",
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "7d5fc3f3-a5c7-489b-be9e-bf6257cfd99c",
                "type": "mcq",
                "prompt": "A nested loop where the outer runs n times and the inner also runs n times has what time complexity?",
                "options": [
                  "O(n)",
                  "O(n log n)",
                  "O(n²)",
                  "O(2ⁿ)"
                ],
                "correct_index": 2,
                "explanation": "Each of the n outer iterations triggers n inner iterations, giving n × n total work.",
                "concept_tag": "nested-loop-complexity",
                "difficulty": "easy",
                "source": "book",
                "hint": null,
                "model_answer": null
              },
              "id": "40f96260-cc5c-4dcd-b26c-9128e9686d04",
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "d3f0a3cb-cbc9-47a0-a845-12ab39363e2c",
                "type": "open",
                "prompt": "In your own words, explain why O(log n) algorithms scale so dramatically better than O(n) for large inputs. Give one concrete example.",
                "options": null,
                "correct_index": null,
                "explanation": "Multiplying the input size adds only a constant to a logarithmic cost, but multiplies a linear cost.",
                "concept_tag": "logarithmic-scaling",
                "difficulty": "medium",
                "source": "ai",
                "hint": "Consider how binary search handles 10⁶ vs 10⁹ elements — how many more steps does it need?",
                "model_answer": "Logarithmic growth adds a constant number of steps each time input multiplies. Going from a million to a billion elements adds only about ten comparisons to a binary search, while a linear scan does a thousand times more work."
              },
              "id": "17d19915-55ec-4c85-b212-0ce52199e8d8",
              "order": 3
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "fbd2b028-0a92-458d-b8df-11245d2d2062",
                "type": "mcq",
                "prompt": "Which correctly simplifies O(5n³ + 12n² + 100)?",
                "options": [
                  "O(5n³)",
                  "O(n³ + n²)",
                  "O(n³)",
                  "O(n²)"
                ],
                "correct_index": 2,
                "explanation": "Constant factors and lower-order terms are dropped, leaving the dominant term.",
                "concept_tag": "simplifying-big-o",
                "difficulty": "easy",
                "source": "book",
                "hint": null,
                "model_answer": null
              },
              "id": "476cb1a5-9b20-423a-bf82-0b19715d1eeb",
              "order": 4
            },
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "Space complexity follows the same notation. Recursive algorithms consume O(depth) call stack frames. A function that recurses n levels deep without tail-call optimization uses O(n) stack space — even if it does no explicit allocation.",
              "id": "64d31b82-8204-4e19-8386-17ee9bb46441",
              "order": 5
            },
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "Amortized analysis averages cost across a sequence of operations. A dynamic array doubles capacity when full — one O(n) resize — but this is rare. Summing all n appends, total cost is O(n), so each append is O(1) amortized.",
              "id": "110b85d0-d576-4a23-946f-4ee114c85b48",
              "order": 6
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "8ce6d390-d6bd-4a0a-995b-5a6ed510e802",
                "type": "open",
                "prompt": "Why doesn't the occasional O(n) resize contradict the claim that dynamic array append is O(1) amortized?",
                "options": null,
                "correct_index": null,
                "explanation": "The expensive operations are rare enough that their total cost is linear across all appends.",
                "concept_tag": "amortized-analysis",
                "difficulty": "hard",
                "source": "book",
                "hint": "How often does a resize happen relative to the total number of appends that precede it?",
                "model_answer": "Resizes happen at exponentially spaced intervals, so their total cost across n appends is O(n). Spread over n operations that averages to constant time each."
              },
              "id": "6fff8286-cb2e-4931-b93b-1f01224cbac4",
              "order": 7
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "6278e1c9-dd1d-4368-a8b7-dffdbee39702",
          "number": 4,
          "title": "Practice: Analyze the Complexity",
          "estimated_minutes": 12,
          "kind": "practice",
          "concept_tags": [
            "loop-analysis",
            "sequential-vs-nested"
          ],
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "content": "Practice applying complexity analysis to code you have not seen before. For each snippet, identify the dominant operation and count how many times it runs.",
              "id": "b9a397cf-e083-4fe5-a698-6843467dda0f",
              "order": 0
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "715fb817-22e9-4338-b339-82fef7d3760a",
                "type": "mcq",
                "prompt": "A loop that halves n each iteration until n reaches 1 runs how many times?",
                "options": [
                  "n times",
                  "n/2 times",
                  "log₂ n times",
                  "√n times"
                ],
                "correct_index": 2,
                "explanation": "Each iteration halves the remaining size, so the count is the number of halvings needed, which is log₂ n.",
                "concept_tag": "loop-analysis",
                "difficulty": "medium",
                "source": "ai",
                "hint": null,
                "model_answer": null
              },
              "id": "27eda185-da63-436b-8212-77068cf951c0",
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "kind": "question",
              "question": {
                "id": "f3f3974a-ac2a-4857-ab3f-7c65786c7f2c",
                "type": "mcq",
                "prompt": "Two sequential loops, each running n times, give what total complexity?",
                "options": [
                  "O(n²)",
                  "O(2n) = O(n)",
                  "O(n log n)",
                  "O(log n)"
                ],
                "correct_index": 1,
                "explanation": "Sequential work adds rather than multiplies, and constant factors drop.",
                "concept_tag": "sequential-vs-nested",
                "difficulty": "easy",
                "source": "book",
                "hint": null,
                "model_answer": null
              },
              "id": "60a00cbb-ce9d-40b1-a324-0c29d026bc84",
              "order": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
